00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:25.380
Some sort of like turkey sized air fryer.

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:26.595
And let me tell you, man,

00:41:26.595 --> 00:41:29.620
I've never experienced anything like it.

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:32.190
It was probably the best
turkey I've ever had.

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:33.911
Are you bullshitting me right now?

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:37.702
No, it was really amazing.

00:41:37.702 --> 00:41:38.535
What did he do to it?

00:41:38.535 --> 00:41:43.535
You know, I don't even like
turkey, but I changed my mind.

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:45.451
After all these years, changed my mind.

00:41:45.451 --> 00:41:47.310
Well, you don't even eat my turkey.

00:41:47.310 --> 00:41:48.273
I taste it.

00:41:49.242 --> 00:41:51.870
I'm always open to it changing my mind.

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:52.703
But what did he do?

00:41:52.703 --> 00:41:53.650
Tell me what he did.

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:55.670
I just told you.

00:41:55.670 --> 00:41:57.723
He put it in an air fryer.

00:41:59.250 --> 00:42:01.350
'Cause I heard that he was doing some...

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:05.370
The way Jenny described
it was some trial runs

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:06.203
because there was-

00:42:06.203 --> 00:42:08.160
Oh yeah, you had built it up so much

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:09.154
that now he's taking over.
The expectations are

00:42:09.154 --> 00:42:09.987
pretty high.

00:42:09.987 --> 00:42:11.818
Okay, all right, we'll
see about next year, Jeb.

00:42:11.818 --> 00:42:14.647
Yeah, I think at one point he asked,

00:42:14.647 --> 00:42:17.609
"Wait, am I off brand Rhett?"

00:42:17.609 --> 00:42:19.400
Maybe we'll have a turkey tango.

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:21.690
I think you guys need a face off.

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:23.130
Okay, all right.

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:27.750
His turkey was as good as any turkey.

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:28.590
But I really don't,

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:30.940
you know, I don't care enough
about turkey to have a-

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:33.523
But you ate the dark meat, I assume.

00:42:33.523 --> 00:42:34.981
No, I just, I grabbed some of it

00:42:34.981 --> 00:42:38.310
just so I could give you shit about it.

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:39.143
Okay.

00:42:39.143 --> 00:42:40.530
No, it was good.

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:42.423
But I'm just, I'm a ham man.

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:44.910
Yeah, I appreciate that.

00:42:44.910 --> 00:42:46.800
It didn't really...

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:49.080
It didn't really sway me, but it was good.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:49.913
It was good.

00:42:49.913 --> 00:42:50.746
It was fine.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:53.922
The other thing.

00:42:53.922 --> 00:42:55.763
The other thing from you?

00:42:55.763 --> 00:42:56.596
Okay.

00:42:56.596 --> 00:42:58.650
The other Thanksgiving thing.

00:42:58.650 --> 00:42:59.483
Okay, yeah.

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:02.933
I...

00:43:02.933 --> 00:43:05.250
You know, you're a soccer guy.

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:07.620
You know, at least, I
mean, you played soccer.

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:08.670
In fact-

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:09.503
In high school, yeah.

00:43:09.503 --> 00:43:12.480
Just recently you played
soccer on the show.

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:15.780
People seem to be impressed
with your kicking.

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:16.613
Oh yeah, yeah.

00:43:16.613 --> 00:43:17.446
Yeah, yeah.

00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:20.610
So, are you a World Cup guy?

00:43:20.610 --> 00:43:21.443
No, sir.

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:23.850
No, sir.

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:24.683
So, there's no-

00:43:24.683 --> 00:43:27.390
I'm a ham man and more, I like music.

00:43:27.390 --> 00:43:28.223
Okay.

00:43:28.223 --> 00:43:29.430
Well, you don't have to pick one.

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:31.230
I'm into music and ham.

00:43:31.230 --> 00:43:33.210
So, I don't like sports and turkey.

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:36.810
So, there's no, okay, so no
World Cupping has happened?

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:37.643
Nah.

00:43:37.643 --> 00:43:41.490
I mean, I listen to the news headlines,

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:44.883
and if something is headline
worthy, I'll take note.

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:51.630
Well, I had the privilege of,

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:54.930
well, so okay, it's
Friday after Thanksgiving,

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:58.410
and this is the annual State,
Carolina football game,

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:03.221
which NC State and
University of North Carolina

00:44:03.221 --> 00:44:05.070
at Chapel Hill, rivals.

00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:06.150
My wife went to Carolina.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:07.083
I went to State.

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:10.320
There was an opportunity
to go to the game,

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:12.570
but I was like, I don't really
want to go to Chapel Hill

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:13.500
and watch State get beat.

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:14.610
That was kind of what I was thinking

00:44:14.610 --> 00:44:16.320
because, you know, State's playing

00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:18.660
with their fourth string quarterback

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:20.758
and like we've been
through three quarterbacks

00:44:20.758 --> 00:44:23.430
to get to the guy, the fourth string.

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:24.263
Oh wow.

00:44:24.263 --> 00:44:25.096
The scout team quarterback.

00:44:25.096 --> 00:44:26.400
Yeah, don't go.

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:28.200
And I don't wanna be in Chapel Hill

00:44:28.200 --> 00:44:30.750
and people know I'm a State
fan if I get recognized

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:33.240
and I'm sitting there watching
my team lose in public.

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:34.640
You know, it's not my thing.

00:44:35.678 --> 00:44:37.680
It's kinda like getting
recognized at the ER.

00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:39.330
I had a couple pictures.

00:44:39.330 --> 00:44:40.163
Oh, good.

00:44:40.163 --> 00:44:40.996
Yeah.

00:44:40.996 --> 00:44:42.990
And the...

00:44:42.990 --> 00:44:46.623
But Locke and then my two
nephews went to the game.

00:44:47.580 --> 00:44:48.922
And I was like, well,
I'm gonna watch the game,

00:44:48.922 --> 00:44:53.255
but I'm going over to
Jessie's sister's house,

00:44:53.255 --> 00:44:55.794
and they're very excited
about the World Cup.

00:44:55.794 --> 00:44:58.380
Like, this is, I mean, I
didn't really expect it.

00:44:58.380 --> 00:45:01.960
I mean, I'm like, if a
game's on, I'll watch it.

00:45:01.960 --> 00:45:04.488
If the US is playing, I'll watch it,

00:45:04.488 --> 00:45:07.950
but I'm not gonna make an appointment.

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:08.783
Right.

00:45:08.783 --> 00:45:09.616
It's just not my thing.

00:45:09.616 --> 00:45:10.945
I call it soccer, you
know what I'm saying?

00:45:10.945 --> 00:45:12.303
I don't call it football.

00:45:13.140 --> 00:45:14.133
I call it soccer.

00:45:16.486 --> 00:45:20.250
But okay, US is, okay,
they're gonna play England.

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:21.210
This is a big deal.

00:45:21.210 --> 00:45:24.330
Our team's really young, and
England's really good and like,

00:45:24.330 --> 00:45:26.228
so okay, we'll watch it.

00:45:26.228 --> 00:45:28.110
But I'm thinking the whole time like,

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:30.060
but that State, Carolina
game is like coming on

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:32.490
before this game's over.

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:35.880
And again, I know, this
is an interest, I had...

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:39.240
There's this soccer versus
American football conversation,

00:45:39.240 --> 00:45:41.976
which the only people on the
face of the planet who think

00:45:41.976 --> 00:45:46.110
that American football is better
than soccer are Americans.

00:45:46.110 --> 00:45:46.943
Right?

00:45:46.943 --> 00:45:47.776
Yeah, right.

00:45:47.776 --> 00:45:48.609
And there's a lot of Americans

00:45:48.609 --> 00:45:49.770
who agree with the rest of the world.

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:51.810
It's like the metric system.

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:52.643
Okay.

00:45:53.557 --> 00:45:54.750
And I think that's a good analogy

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:56.970
because I'm not saying it makes any sense.

00:45:56.970 --> 00:45:59.550
I mean, clearly the metric
system is a superior system

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:04.550
than the king's system
of pounds and inches.

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:07.472
But I just gotta tell you,

00:46:07.472 --> 00:46:12.472
I had the distinct experience
of watching the game,

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:16.650
the soccer game, and then the
football game back to back.

00:46:16.650 --> 00:46:17.643
And for me,

00:46:18.721 --> 00:46:23.670
it's like all this excitement
around the US and England,

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:28.530
and then we're supposed to
walk away from this satisfied

00:46:28.530 --> 00:46:30.870
that the score was zero to zero.

00:46:30.870 --> 00:46:32.190
Wow.

00:46:32.190 --> 00:46:36.060
And we're happy about
this and it's awesome.

00:46:36.060 --> 00:46:36.969
Yeah, I don't-

00:46:36.969 --> 00:46:39.360
And then we changed the channel.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:42.180
We began watching State
and Carolina play football.

00:46:42.180 --> 00:46:45.660
And it goes into double overtime.

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:46.710
And it was crazy.

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:48.630
It was just crazy back and forth.

00:46:48.630 --> 00:46:49.740
Jenna was watching it.

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:50.573
Oh yeah.

00:46:50.573 --> 00:46:51.406
Oh yeah.

00:46:51.406 --> 00:46:53.130
It was a good game.
It was like...

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:54.960
You know, and I'm like, what,

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:56.751
this dude Finley whose brother played

00:46:56.751 --> 00:47:00.180
at State a few years ago,
who's not supposed to be great,

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:01.770
is throwing these crazy passes,

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:05.100
and then it gets down to the
end and they scored touchdown,

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:06.333
but then it gets ruled incomplete.

00:47:06.333 --> 00:47:07.791
But then it was zero seconds.

00:47:07.791 --> 00:47:11.520
I'm watching the game
with my father-in-law

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:15.660
who, you know, went to UNC
undergrad and UNC dental school.

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:16.523
Huge Carolina fan.

00:47:16.523 --> 00:47:19.020
And so, and we're the only two
who really care in the house,

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:19.853
so we're the only ones

00:47:19.853 --> 00:47:21.660
who are there the whole
time yelling at each other.

00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:22.980
He's hitting me.

00:47:22.980 --> 00:47:24.210
You know, like, what I mean,

00:47:24.210 --> 00:47:26.490
like they score, he like
punches me in the shoulder.

00:47:26.490 --> 00:47:27.323
That kind of thing.

00:47:27.323 --> 00:47:28.329
We have that kind of relationship.

00:47:28.329 --> 00:47:29.162
Don't do that.

00:47:29.162 --> 00:47:30.480
I'm not gonna do it to you.

00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:33.578
And none of this was, just so you know,

00:47:33.578 --> 00:47:36.840
none of this was happening
during USA versus England.

00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:38.310
There was no punching in the shoulder.

00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:39.570
There was no standing.

00:47:39.570 --> 00:47:40.403
I'm just saying.

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:43.620
I enjoyed it, you know?

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:44.820
I think we're playing,

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:47.070
today I think we're playing
Iran when we're recording this.

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:47.933
And probably right now.

00:47:47.933 --> 00:47:50.430
I mean, I'm interested
to know what happens.

00:47:50.430 --> 00:47:52.530
I would watch it if I didn't have to work.

00:47:54.168 --> 00:47:55.770
But goes into double overtime.

00:47:55.770 --> 00:47:56.940
State wins.

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:58.557
I'm freaking, this is amazing.

00:47:58.557 --> 00:48:02.160
And it's awesome to be able to
watch it with a Carolina fan

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:04.203
and watch them sort of shrivel.

00:48:05.100 --> 00:48:06.960
And then we turn the channel

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:09.180
to the Carolina basketball game.

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:11.160
Number one, Carolina is,

00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:13.168
like literally gets beat by Iowa State.

00:48:13.168 --> 00:48:14.097
Like, we turn it over

00:48:14.097 --> 00:48:16.334
and like 30 seconds left
on the clock and they lose.

00:48:17.292 --> 00:48:19.625
And it was just like this,
and again, this is immature.

00:48:19.625 --> 00:48:22.230
This is not something
that I wanna promote.

00:48:22.230 --> 00:48:24.549
I'm just letting you know that it was in-

00:48:24.549 --> 00:48:25.890
You've just chosen to talk about it.

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:30.450
It was insanely satisfying
to watch Carolina get beat

00:48:30.450 --> 00:48:32.010
and State beat them in football

00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:34.460
and then went on that
night to win in basketball.

00:48:35.550 --> 00:48:37.290
I'm not saying that we'll
beat 'em in basketball later.

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:41.390
But I just had a like
a one-to-one experience

00:48:41.390 --> 00:48:43.920
of soccer versus American football.

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:45.840
And my suspicions were confirmed

00:48:45.840 --> 00:48:48.630
that I'm significantly more entertained

00:48:48.630 --> 00:48:49.520
by American football.

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:52.380
I thought maybe I would
get a different perspective

00:48:52.380 --> 00:48:55.682
from a soccer guy, but I
guess you're just a ham guy.

00:48:55.682 --> 00:48:56.890
Yeah.

00:48:56.890 --> 00:49:00.600
I was looking forward
to you defending soccer.

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:04.080
Well, you know, it's
quite an aerobic workout.

00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:05.430
They're constantly running.

00:49:07.097 --> 00:49:09.055
Hey, I was thinking that.

00:49:09.055 --> 00:49:11.727
I was thinking these
dudes are running around

00:49:11.727 --> 00:49:12.990
for 45 minutes.

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:14.100
Oh yeah.

00:49:14.100 --> 00:49:15.150
And like the football players are

00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:16.110
like getting a lot of breaks.

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:18.270
It's like, oh, I just played defense.

00:49:18.270 --> 00:49:19.103
A lot of kneeling.

00:49:19.103 --> 00:49:19.969
I'm just playing defense.

00:49:19.969 --> 00:49:20.802
I mean, what if-

00:49:20.802 --> 00:49:22.650
You just play offense and
we take a lot of breaks,

00:49:22.650 --> 00:49:24.218
and you stand around and there's timeout.

00:49:24.218 --> 00:49:25.551
Half the team watches half the team.

00:49:25.551 --> 00:49:26.400
How do you run around for 45?

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:28.342
It's incredibly impressive.

00:49:28.342 --> 00:49:30.019
It's incredibly impressive.

00:49:30.019 --> 00:49:31.680
But it's just the goal.

00:49:31.680 --> 00:49:33.510
Something, the goal needs to be bigger.

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:35.460
Something needs to change.

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:37.650
Just something needs
to change a little bit.

00:49:37.650 --> 00:49:38.793
Like, the goal.

00:49:38.793 --> 00:49:40.251
Either let's get rid of the keeper

00:49:40.251 --> 00:49:41.584
or let's make the goal bigger.

00:49:41.584 --> 00:49:45.417
We need one of those options
to happen and then America

00:49:45.417 --> 00:49:47.301
will be all over it
Trap doors, man.

00:49:47.301 --> 00:49:48.134
Trap doors.

00:49:48.134 --> 00:49:51.217
What if we invented a new
thing called American soccer?

00:49:51.217 --> 00:49:53.760
We don't have to get the
rest of the world to change.

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:54.593
Hold on.

00:49:54.593 --> 00:49:57.180
Why would it be called American soccer?

00:49:57.180 --> 00:49:59.730
Well, just to, it might
be a little redundant.

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:00.882
American football.

00:50:02.850 --> 00:50:04.410
I think we just call it soccer

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:07.110
and then parenthesis
American, just to remind you.

00:50:07.110 --> 00:50:07.983
Because what I'm saying is

00:50:07.983 --> 00:50:09.510
that the rules are gonna be different,

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:11.005
and we're gonna have a giant goal.

00:50:11.005 --> 00:50:14.463
In fact, one whole side of
the field is just a goal.

00:50:14.463 --> 00:50:17.190
Like, when you get down to
that side and it's just a goal.

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:18.023
It's just a net.

00:50:18.023 --> 00:50:18.870
It's like a gutter.

00:50:18.870 --> 00:50:20.920
If you can get the ball in
the gutter, it's a point.

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:22.770
I think you're describing an end zone.

00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:23.603
Exactly!

00:50:24.711 --> 00:50:27.030
I just created an end zone.

00:50:27.030 --> 00:50:29.040
This is how American football got created

00:50:29.040 --> 00:50:30.660
'cause they were like, how
do we make this easier?

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:32.910
What if you could just
carry it across this line?

00:50:32.910 --> 00:50:34.410
What if you could touch it with your hands

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:36.569
and run it across this line?

00:50:36.569 --> 00:50:37.620
We don't have to do that.

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:39.390
But let's just make the end a gutter.

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:41.500
And if you could get into
the gutter, it's a goal.

00:50:41.500 --> 00:50:45.208
Games will be like 48 to 45.

00:50:45.208 --> 00:50:46.923
Everybody will be watching.

00:50:50.070 --> 00:50:52.082
Hey man, I don't wanna
join you on this hill

00:50:52.082 --> 00:50:56.430
because I don't know,
shots are gonna be fired.

00:50:56.430 --> 00:50:57.263
I know.

00:50:57.263 --> 00:50:58.170
It's not a popular opinion.

00:50:58.170 --> 00:50:59.220
I appreciate it.

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:00.053
I think that,

00:51:00.053 --> 00:51:02.460
I understand that soccer
is like every man's game.

00:51:02.460 --> 00:51:04.950
It's like, it's so much
more easily accessible.

00:51:04.950 --> 00:51:05.783
All you need is a ball,

00:51:05.783 --> 00:51:08.370
and you can just start playing
it in your neighborhood.

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:09.270
I'm an outdoors man.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:10.890
Your hair's looking a
little like you touched it

00:51:10.890 --> 00:51:11.940
with your left hand.

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:12.773
I know.

00:51:12.773 --> 00:51:13.606
It's like...

00:51:14.523 --> 00:51:15.660
Does it go this way?

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:16.920
You'll figure it.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:18.620
You'll figure it out.
This way.

00:51:19.590 --> 00:51:21.030
I'm an outdoors man.

00:51:21.030 --> 00:51:21.863
That's what I do with my time.

00:51:21.863 --> 00:51:23.280
Yeah, you spend time out on the bike.

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:24.630
Me and the boys

00:51:24.630 --> 00:51:29.630
from like the Saturday before
Thanksgiving to Wednesday,

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.670
so Saturday, Sunday, Monday, Tuesday,

00:51:32.670 --> 00:51:37.670
four days, five nights we
went on a van camping trip.

00:51:38.250 --> 00:51:41.040
You know, I rented the Sprinter van,

00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:42.240
the one that me and Christy went on.

00:51:42.240 --> 00:51:43.590
And we actually,

00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:47.857
I took the boys on pretty much
the same trip that I took,

00:51:47.857 --> 00:51:51.000
that Christy and I went
on during the pandemic

00:51:51.000 --> 00:51:52.200
when we went to, you know,

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:54.606
when we went to Zion and Bryce Canyon,

00:51:54.606 --> 00:51:58.950
and then we had that weird encounter-

00:51:58.950 --> 00:51:59.880
With the serial killer.

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:00.713
With the guy, yeah,

00:52:00.713 --> 00:52:04.938
with the serial killer who
wanted to show her his photos

00:52:04.938 --> 00:52:07.233
so she could vote on them.

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:10.469
Nothing that scary happened.

00:52:10.469 --> 00:52:13.924
But when you go in November,

00:52:13.924 --> 00:52:18.300
which was weeks later than
when Christy and I went,

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:20.640
I think maybe we went in September.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:22.140
Little nippy out there, huh?

00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:25.620
But I wanted to take the
boys back to the Narrows,

00:52:25.620 --> 00:52:29.400
which, I mean, the
famous hikes at Zion are,

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:30.600
you got Angels Landing.

00:52:30.600 --> 00:52:32.130
Zion is so beautiful.

00:52:32.130 --> 00:52:33.393
I highly recommend it.

00:52:35.370 --> 00:52:37.340
Angels Landing is like, you got this...

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:41.460
What's it called?

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:42.540
It's not a cavern.

00:52:42.540 --> 00:52:43.373
Canyon.

00:52:43.373 --> 00:52:44.550
Good gosh.

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:45.873
I didn't hit my head.

00:52:47.515 --> 00:52:49.080
And then in the middle of it,

00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:50.520
there's like this rock formation

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:52.290
that you can kind of hike out to

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:54.597
that's like you gotta use
these special cables and stuff,

00:52:54.597 --> 00:52:56.400
and you gotta get a
permit to go out there.

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:57.420
I really wanna do that,

00:52:57.420 --> 00:52:59.596
but like there's no way that
I'm doing that with the boys.

00:52:59.596 --> 00:53:02.823
And like, I didn't think
I was ready for it anyway.

00:53:04.429 --> 00:53:06.630
So, we did the hike
that Christy and I did,

00:53:06.630 --> 00:53:08.760
which is an absolutely amazing hike.

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:13.428
The Virgin River has carved this canyon,

00:53:13.428 --> 00:53:18.123
and then at the far end of the park,

00:53:20.670 --> 00:53:25.320
you can hike a trail that
then you get in the water

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:28.320
and then you're hiking in the river,

00:53:28.320 --> 00:53:29.610
which when Christy and I did it,

00:53:29.610 --> 00:53:34.610
it was like ankle to at
some places knee depth,

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:36.300
and it was cold at the time.

00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:37.620
So, I did my research and I was like,

00:53:37.620 --> 00:53:39.450
I really want the boys to experience this.

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:41.190
Christy really built it up for Lando

00:53:41.190 --> 00:53:44.160
and like he was excited about
it, about going one day.

00:53:44.160 --> 00:53:45.990
So, I was like, "Boys, we can do this."

00:53:45.990 --> 00:53:47.220
And it's gonna be more of an adventure

00:53:47.220 --> 00:53:51.123
because yes, the water
could be like 27 degrees.

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:53.460
That's pretty dang cold.

00:53:53.460 --> 00:53:54.783
That's pretty dang cold.

00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:59.400
And now I'm questioning,
is that even right?

00:53:59.400 --> 00:54:01.080
Definitely in the 30s.

00:54:01.080 --> 00:54:03.127
I mean, 32 is freezing,
but if it's moving-

00:54:03.127 --> 00:54:05.130
If it's moving, it doesn't freeze.

00:54:05.130 --> 00:54:08.850
Yeah, so there's like,
there's ice and snow around.

00:54:08.850 --> 00:54:12.150
It's not snowing there,
or it wasn't predicted to,

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:15.120
but we had to rent special equipment,

00:54:15.120 --> 00:54:20.120
like special pants, like dry suit pants,

00:54:20.292 --> 00:54:23.730
so that you wear a couple
of layers underneath it

00:54:23.730 --> 00:54:26.010
and then you pull these like,
it's kinda like waiters,

00:54:26.010 --> 00:54:27.630
but they're really tight at the ankles,

00:54:27.630 --> 00:54:28.860
so they don't let water in.

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:31.020
So, it's like a dry suit bottom.

00:54:31.020 --> 00:54:33.896
And then like special hiking
boots and neoprene socks,

00:54:33.896 --> 00:54:36.729
so that your feet do get wet.

00:54:36.729 --> 00:54:40.200
Like, potentially completely wet.

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:42.630
But then just like a wet suit,

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:44.853
the water inside of the
neoprene socks is heated

00:54:44.853 --> 00:54:48.311
by your circulation and
your feet stay warm.

00:54:48.311 --> 00:54:51.090
Do you rent these shoes and everything?

00:54:51.090 --> 00:54:52.140
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:52.980
You rent all of that.

00:54:52.980 --> 00:54:54.390
A number of people were doing it.

00:54:54.390 --> 00:54:56.726
If you go in November, you
can pretty much have the place

00:54:56.726 --> 00:54:58.559
to yourself is what I read.

00:54:58.559 --> 00:55:00.450
Because it's cold?

00:55:00.450 --> 00:55:01.500
Because it's so cold.

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:02.970
People don't want to do that.

00:55:02.970 --> 00:55:05.100
Like, a lot of times you go to Zion,

00:55:05.100 --> 00:55:07.410
and from the pictures I've seen,

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:10.050
it's like there's just a lot
of people in that canyon.

00:55:10.050 --> 00:55:10.895
And does it close

00:55:10.895 --> 00:55:11.728
at some point?
The hike is called

00:55:11.728 --> 00:55:13.311
the Narrows.

00:55:13.311 --> 00:55:15.930
Like, is it almost about to close?

00:55:15.930 --> 00:55:18.213
I don't recall.
Like, Yosemite closes.

00:55:19.080 --> 00:55:21.300
I don't think it gets, it
doesn't get snow like that,

00:55:21.300 --> 00:55:24.518
so I don't think it does.

00:55:24.518 --> 00:55:27.000
But don't quote me on that.

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:29.340
But we did that.

00:55:29.340 --> 00:55:30.240
It was quite an adventure.

00:55:30.240 --> 00:55:31.980
Because it was Thanksgiving week,

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:33.450
there were almost as many people

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:34.767
as when Christy and I went during COVID.

00:55:34.767 --> 00:55:37.782
So, it was like, it was
still very manageable.

00:55:37.782 --> 00:55:39.930
But it was quite an adventure.

00:55:39.930 --> 00:55:44.640
I mean, it was five
hours, a five hour hike

00:55:44.640 --> 00:55:46.770
'cause we went basically all the way in

00:55:46.770 --> 00:55:48.810
to where they recommend turning around

00:55:48.810 --> 00:55:50.613
and then coming back out.

00:55:51.507 --> 00:55:53.580
You rent the same van?

00:55:53.580 --> 00:55:54.413
Yeah.

00:55:54.413 --> 00:55:55.470
And I was impressed with Lando.

00:55:55.470 --> 00:55:57.720
You know, with his height having to trudge

00:55:57.720 --> 00:56:01.230
through that water, he's
encountered a lot more resistance

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:02.220
than Lincoln and I are,

00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:04.680
'cause, you know, Lincoln's
freaking taller than me now.

00:56:04.680 --> 00:56:05.513
Oh yeah.

00:56:05.513 --> 00:56:06.630
Six freaking one.

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.224
Crazy.

00:56:08.224 --> 00:56:09.932
Lincoln may end up being the tallest

00:56:09.932 --> 00:56:12.570
of all our kids.
The tallest Neal.

00:56:12.570 --> 00:56:13.680
I mean, Shepherd's-

00:56:13.680 --> 00:56:15.450
How tall is Locke?

00:56:15.450 --> 00:56:16.500
5'11".

00:56:16.500 --> 00:56:17.400
Oh, really?

00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:18.233
Yeah.

00:56:19.350 --> 00:56:20.400
Yeah, that's crazy.

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:21.510
I mean-

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:23.804
And Shepherd is like 5'10 and 1/2",

00:56:23.804 --> 00:56:25.860
but he's only 14, so he's probably,

00:56:25.860 --> 00:56:28.170
I don't know, it could be weird.

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:29.010
Gonna start some rumors.

00:56:29.010 --> 00:56:32.160
You were 6'4" when you
were Shepherd's age.

00:56:32.160 --> 00:56:34.110
Oh yeah, 6'4" in eighth grade.

00:56:34.110 --> 00:56:34.950
That's crazy, man.

00:56:34.950 --> 00:56:37.980
Thankfully my kids did
not get all of that.

00:56:37.980 --> 00:56:42.483
Yeah, there was a balancing
factor called Jessie.

00:56:44.130 --> 00:56:46.080
So, but Lando did great.

00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:49.259
And like, you know, we
got some good pictures,

00:56:49.259 --> 00:56:51.120
had a good time.

00:56:51.120 --> 00:56:54.499
You know, it was, it got
pretty cold at night.

00:56:54.499 --> 00:56:55.980
But you got the van.

00:56:55.980 --> 00:56:57.300
But there's heat in the van,

00:56:57.300 --> 00:56:59.760
so it's like, it's definitely glamping.

00:56:59.760 --> 00:57:02.965
And so yeah, we had a good time.

00:57:02.965 --> 00:57:07.593
No weird run-ins with strange people.

00:57:09.900 --> 00:57:11.030
And then...

00:57:13.830 --> 00:57:15.513
I'm all of a sudden so tired.

00:57:16.380 --> 00:57:19.170
I feel like, I feel like...

00:57:19.170 --> 00:57:20.430
You might be one of those people

00:57:20.430 --> 00:57:23.031
that keeps a part of their
brain in their shoulder.

00:57:23.031 --> 00:57:24.900
It could have been what
that little bump is.

00:57:24.900 --> 00:57:28.072
Like, last night at eight
o'clock, I was like,

00:57:28.072 --> 00:57:29.790
"I think I'm gonna turn in."

00:57:29.790 --> 00:57:30.623
And Christy's like,

00:57:30.623 --> 00:57:32.707
"Oh no, we need to
watch 'The White Lotus.'

00:57:32.707 --> 00:57:34.350
"It's only eight o'clock."

00:57:34.350 --> 00:57:36.112
Good lord, what episode are you on?

00:57:36.112 --> 00:57:37.290
I'm caught up, but-

00:57:37.290 --> 00:57:38.123
Oh gosh.

00:57:38.123 --> 00:57:42.900
I was like, I think that my
body is rebuilding a shoulder.

00:57:42.900 --> 00:57:44.250
Yeah, there's a lot of, yeah.

00:57:44.250 --> 00:57:45.083
You know?

00:57:45.083 --> 00:57:46.980
Making connections that
you don't typically make.

00:57:46.980 --> 00:57:49.140
And then you can't make
connections in your brain.

00:57:49.140 --> 00:57:53.880
It's just like my bone is
sewing itself back together.

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:54.713
Don't think about it.

00:57:54.713 --> 00:57:56.610
It'll make you faint.
Now I'm gonna faint again.

00:57:56.610 --> 00:58:01.610
But we had a really good
time, and it was special.

00:58:01.783 --> 00:58:03.477
I don't think that me

00:58:03.477 --> 00:58:05.400
and the boys have ever done a camping trip

00:58:05.400 --> 00:58:08.179
just the three of us that we could recall.

00:58:08.179 --> 00:58:10.030
I'm jealous, man, because I, you know,

00:58:10.030 --> 00:58:13.530
I was gonna do a shorter trip-

00:58:13.530 --> 00:58:14.363
With your boys.

00:58:14.363 --> 00:58:16.590
With my boys, but with our plans changing.

00:58:16.590 --> 00:58:18.750
Lando learned how to whistle.

00:58:18.750 --> 00:58:19.583
Oh.

00:58:19.583 --> 00:58:21.690
You know, we're sitting by
the campfire the first night

00:58:21.690 --> 00:58:22.523
and he's like,

00:58:23.512 --> 00:58:25.620
"I'd really like to learn how to whistle."

00:58:25.620 --> 00:58:28.357
I was like, "Son, this
is the perfect place

00:58:28.357 --> 00:58:29.857
"and the perfect time.

00:58:29.857 --> 00:58:33.547
"We ain't got nothing to do
except sit here by a fire

00:58:33.547 --> 00:58:34.530
"and learn to whistle."

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:36.690
What's a whistle lesson involve?

00:58:36.690 --> 00:58:37.913
It's you whistling.

00:58:37.913 --> 00:58:39.397
Just saying, "Well, just "

00:58:39.397 --> 00:58:40.230
"Just do this."

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:41.063
"You know, just "

00:58:41.063 --> 00:58:42.245
"Just do this.

00:58:42.245 --> 00:58:43.078
"Keep trying."

00:58:43.078 --> 00:58:43.911
Now you can't whistle.

00:58:43.911 --> 00:58:44.760
Now change it

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:46.620
Okay, you can whistle,
but you're not good at it.

00:58:46.620 --> 00:58:47.453
I can whistle.

00:58:47.453 --> 00:58:48.423
And I got two notes.

00:58:52.560 --> 00:58:53.850
I've got four notes now.

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:55.492
Can you do it without
moving your eyebrows?

00:58:58.080 --> 00:58:59.430
Whistle a tune.

00:58:59.430 --> 00:59:01.574
Here, I'm gonna hum a tune.

00:59:01.574 --> 00:59:02.407
No, no, no.

00:59:02.407 --> 00:59:03.720
No, not a random tune.

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:04.890
No, that was sitting
on the dock of the bay.

00:59:04.890 --> 00:59:06.033
Match my tune.

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:13.030
Nope, that wasn't-

00:59:13.030 --> 00:59:15.613
I've got like, there's like
three tones that I can do.

00:59:17.760 --> 00:59:18.933
Yeah, I mean, listen, you
just, but you just accused-

00:59:18.933 --> 00:59:21.030
Is that from "The Little Mermaid?"

00:59:21.030 --> 00:59:23.100
You just accused me of
not being able to whistle

00:59:23.100 --> 00:59:24.600
and I whistled immediately.

00:59:24.600 --> 00:59:25.573
I just gotta say.

00:59:25.573 --> 00:59:27.180
I mean, I'm not good at it.

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:28.533
You're bad at it.

00:59:28.533 --> 00:59:31.866
Okay, well, bad at it would
be not being able to do it.

00:59:31.866 --> 00:59:33.090
No.

00:59:33.090 --> 00:59:35.130
You're bad at whistling.

00:59:35.130 --> 00:59:37.365
If you can't whistle,
then you can't whistle.

00:59:37.365 --> 00:59:39.073
I just think it's good for you to say

00:59:39.073 --> 00:59:40.410
that you're bad at something.

00:59:40.410 --> 00:59:41.243
Well-

00:59:41.243 --> 00:59:42.150
Just say you're bad at whistling.

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:44.550
I just think I could be a lot worse.

00:59:44.550 --> 00:59:45.613
Why can't you just say-

00:59:45.613 --> 00:59:47.197
If there's a spectrum of whistling-

00:59:47.197 --> 00:59:48.747
"I'm not good at whistling."

00:59:48.747 --> 00:59:50.310
I'm not good at whistling.

00:59:50.310 --> 00:59:52.820
But a bad whistler is
too strong of a word.

00:59:52.820 --> 00:59:54.960
If there's a spectrum of whistling-

00:59:54.960 --> 00:59:56.100
Give me your best whistle.

00:59:56.100 --> 00:59:56.933
I'm on the lower,

00:59:56.933 --> 01:00:00.000
I am in the 50th
percentile or lower, easy.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:01.453
I would say that I'm in
the 30th percentile of-

01:00:01.453 --> 01:00:03.780
Well, then you're a bad whistler.

01:00:03.780 --> 01:00:06.709
Like, what is the percentile
of a bad whistler?

01:00:06.709 --> 01:00:08.542
15 or down.

01:00:08.542 --> 01:00:10.353
Give me your best whistle.

01:00:12.360 --> 01:00:13.416
That's bad, dude.

01:00:13.416 --> 01:00:14.520
But I'm better than-

01:00:14.520 --> 01:00:17.370
I'm hearing wind more than whistle.

01:00:17.370 --> 01:00:19.370
If you take everybody who can whistle,

01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:23.670
I'm better than 30% of them.

01:00:23.670 --> 01:00:24.570
No, you're not.

01:00:24.570 --> 01:00:26.820
Okay, well, we're gonna
have to figure out a way

01:00:26.820 --> 01:00:27.653
to do that.

01:00:27.653 --> 01:00:28.530
Like, when

01:00:28.530 --> 01:00:29.663
When you hear that kind of stuff-

01:00:29.663 --> 01:00:31.470
That's not whistling.

01:00:31.470 --> 01:00:35.520
No, but that's 70% of your whistle.

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:36.412
Oh no, it's not.

01:00:39.703 --> 01:00:41.286
You're trying so hard.

01:00:41.286 --> 01:00:43.140
That's not that bad, man.

01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:44.327
Just be bad at it, dude.

01:00:44.327 --> 01:00:48.420
Okay, if the lower 50% is the bad half,

01:00:48.420 --> 01:00:51.750
I'm on the bad half, but there
are worse whistlers than me,

01:00:51.750 --> 01:00:52.583
without a doubt.

01:00:52.583 --> 01:00:54.600
Name something you're bad at.

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:57.090
And say it this way, "I am bad at blank."

01:00:57.090 --> 01:00:58.533
I am bad at being short.

01:01:00.750 --> 01:01:02.100
You see?

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:02.973
You see this?

01:01:04.440 --> 01:01:05.463
Do you hear this?

01:01:06.750 --> 01:01:09.090
I am bad at not having a beard.

01:01:09.090 --> 01:01:09.923
Lando-

01:01:09.923 --> 01:01:11.490
I will...

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:13.058
Lando is a bad whistler.

01:01:13.058 --> 01:01:14.141
But you know what?

01:01:14.141 --> 01:01:15.349
He's just learning.

01:01:15.349 --> 01:01:16.432
He's just learning.
He's just learning.

01:01:16.432 --> 01:01:18.765
Did he get better than
me during his lesson?

01:01:18.765 --> 01:01:19.598
Yes.

01:01:19.598 --> 01:01:20.431
Oh, come on.

01:01:20.431 --> 01:01:21.410
Well, he's got a Neal mouth.

01:01:22.500 --> 01:01:23.347
A lot of it's the tongue.

01:01:23.347 --> 01:01:25.305
When you really try to
teach somebody to whistle,

01:01:25.305 --> 01:01:27.330
you really discover a
lot of it's the tongue.

01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:28.650
Here's the thing.

01:01:28.650 --> 01:01:29.804
Now in the golf world,

01:01:29.804 --> 01:01:31.800
there's something called
a handicap, right?

01:01:31.800 --> 01:01:32.633
And I think in the whistle world-

01:01:32.633 --> 01:01:34.140
Yeah, change the subject
to something you know.

01:01:34.140 --> 01:01:36.750
No, in the whistle world,
you have a mouth handicap.

01:01:36.750 --> 01:01:38.970
If you have my mouth,

01:01:38.970 --> 01:01:41.302
you're starting off so far
behind with whistle ability

01:01:41.302 --> 01:01:43.885
that I think for everyone
who has a mouth like me-

01:01:43.885 --> 01:01:45.000
You're saying it's so small?

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:46.980
I am in the 90th percentile of people

01:01:46.980 --> 01:01:47.813
